+# Summary
+
+**Changed:** implemented `satisfies()` and `max_satisfying()` in `depres/ranges.py` per `docs/RANGES.md`. They cover `||` sets, primitive operators (with optional space after the operator), partial versions and X-ranges, hyphen ranges, tilde, caret, and the prerelease rule. Invalid ranges or versions raise `ValueError`. `max_satisfying` returns the original string of the highest match, or `None`. Nothing else was modified.
+
+**Verified:**
+- `python -m unittest`: 8 tests ran, all OK (previously all errored).
+- An ad-hoc script with about 40 cases taken from the examples in `RANGES.md` (partial-version table, hyphen, tilde, caret, prerelease, `v` prefix, build metadata) all gave the expected results. It also checked that `>=`, `1.x.3`, `~`, `^`, `1.2.3 -`, `A - B - C` and `a` raise `ValueError`. `max_satisfying` was checked on an empty list and on versions with build metadata. This script is not committed as tests.
+
+**Not verified / unsure (the doc is silent):**
+- `>*` and `<*` match nothing, and `>=*`, `<=*` and `=*` match everything (npm behaviour).
+- When two versions compare equal (differing only in build metadata), `max_satisfying` returns the first one.
+- `~>` is not supported.
+- Prereleases are only allowed on full versions (`1.2-beta` is invalid).
+- I did not add the extra cases to `tests/test_ranges.py`.
